* performance hunting
  after writing a timing library and measuring the execution time of movegen functions it has become apparent that attack, pin and check generation accounts 
  roughtly 1/4 of the time spent, adding to the movestack took another 1/4 while leaving 1/2 of the time spent unaccounted for 

  KingMoveFunction took 28.174798164604752 ns on average over 206604 trials
  ComputePinMasks took 142.20679173684923 ns on average over 206604 trials
  ComputeCheckMasks took 109.27323285125166 ns on average over 206604 trials
  AddToStack218 took 31.64060968737194 ns on average over 3304054 trials
  MakeAllMoves took 1963.9508964008442 ns on average over 206604 trials
  AttackCreationRook took 24.41499712410059 ns on average over 1446504 trials
  AttackCreationBishop took 25.98000488073651 ns on average over 1446503 trials
  
  KingMoveFunction took 5.821026 ms in total
  ComputePinMasks took 29.380492 ms in total
  ComputeCheckMasks took 22.576287 ms in total
  AddToStack218 took 104.542283 ms in total
  MakeAllMoves took 405.760111 ms in total
  AttackCreationRook took 35.316391 ms in total
  AttackCreationBishop took 37.580155 ms in total

  inside the make all moves function lines we spend first seperate individual bitboards into fixed size arrays of uint64_t 
  in the enemy piece attack generation
  FastStack pieceStack {seperateBitboardFastStackReturn<10>(board.getPiecesByColorConst(isWhiteTurnInner)[i])};
  in the move generation
  FastStack pieceStack {seperateBitboardFastStackReturn<10>(board.getPiecesByColorConst(isWhiteTurn)[i])};
  during pawn promotion
  FastStack promotionSquares {seperateBitboardFastStackReturn<3>(pawnPromotionSquare)};

  the first two instances are executed six times each totalling twelve function calls while the pawn promotion calls are 
  determined by the board state

  the creation and consumption of small small arrays during move generation is completely unecessary and only exists because it is an easier solution than
  including the low level code in the outer loop

  after adding measurement for seperateBitboardFastStackReturn the performance measurement looks like this 

  KingMoveFunction took 5.852041 ms in total
  ComputePinMasks took 50.332673 ms in total
  ComputeCheckMasks took 23.904494 ms in total
  AddToStack218 took 201.622538 ms in total
  MakeAllMoves took 613.279281 ms in total
  AttackCreationRook took 37.633971 ms in total
  AttackCreationBishop took 39.293431 ms in total
  SeperateBitboardFastStack took 110.985519 ms in total

  KingMoveFunction took 28.324916264931947 ns on average over 206604 trials
  ComputePinMasks took 243.61906352248747 ns on average over 206604 trials
  ComputeCheckMasks took 115.7019902809239 ns on average over 206604 trials
  AddToStack218 took 61.022773235546396 ns on average over 3304054 trials
  MakeAllMoves took 2968.380481500842 ns on average over 206604 trials
  AttackCreationRook took 26.01719110351579 ns on average over 1446504 trials
  AttackCreationBishop took 27.164431045079063 ns on average over 1446503 trials
  SeperateBitboardFastStack took 17.332241676080915 ns on average over 6403414 trials

  the overhead for measuring SeperateBitboardFastStack is around 90ms 
  so the function itself isnt taking much cpu time but the overhead of creating and destroying a lot of 
  small arrays probably contiributes significantly to the running time of the program

